# One owner, every path.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES ON ITS OWN: assigns a reviewer. GitHub requests review
# from the owner of every changed path when a pull request opens. That is all.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: gate anything. A CODEOWNERS entry becomes a merge
# requirement only when the `protect-main` ruleset turns on "require review
# from Code Owners", which today it does not — it requires a pull request, the
# `Checks` context, and that the branch be up to date, with zero approvals.
# So this is a review REQUEST, not a gate, until that setting changes.
#
# BEFORE TURNING THAT SETTING ON, know what it costs here. GitHub does not let
# anyone approve their own pull request, and every PR in this repo is opened by
# the account this file names. Enforced code-owner review would leave each one
# unmergeable except through the admin bypass the ruleset already grants — a
# gate whose only outcome is that it gets bypassed, which is worse than no gate
# because it reads like one. It becomes real the day a second person can
# approve.
#
# That is also why the repo's own docs say CI is the merge gate: there is no
# human reviewer, so the PR body is written as the durable record rather than a
# request for attention. See ../CLAUDE.md and the design-system-pr skill.
